Here are both approaches for .NET MAUI Shell navigation:

**1. Register the route**
```csharp
// AppShell.xaml.cs
Routing.RegisterRoute("productdetails", typeof(ProductDetailsPage));
```

**2. Navigate – string parameter**
```csharp
// ProductListViewModel.cs
[RelayCommand]
async Task GoToDetails(Product product)
{
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync($"productdetails?id={product.Id}");
}
```

**3. Navigate – passing the full object**
```csharp
[RelayCommand]
async Task GoToDetailsWithObject(Product product)
{
    var parameters = new Dictionary<string, object>
    {
        { "product", product }
    };
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("productdetails", parameters);
}
```

**4. Receive data with `IQueryAttributable`**
```csharp
// ProductDetailsViewModel.cs
public partial class ProductDetailsViewModel : ObservableObject, IQueryAttributable
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    string productId;

    [ObservableProperty]
    Product product;

    public void ApplyQueryAttributes(IDictionary<string, object> query)
    {
        // String parameter arrives as string
        if (query.TryGetValue("id", out var id))
            ProductId = id.ToString();

        // Complex object arrives as-is (passed by reference)
        if (query.TryGetValue("product", out var obj) && obj is Product p)
            Product = p;
    }
}
```

**5. Wire the ViewModel to the page**
```csharp
// ProductDetailsPage.xaml.cs
public ProductDetailsPage(ProductDetailsViewModel vm)
{
    InitializeComponent();
    BindingContext = vm;
}
```

**Key differences:**

| | String (`?id=42`) | Dictionary object |
|---|---|---|
| **Data** | Primitives only, URL-encoded | Any object, passed by ref |
| **In `ApplyQueryAttributes`** | Value is `string` | Value is the original type |
| **Back-navigation** | Re-applied from URL | Lost on back nav unless cached |

Use the string approach for simple IDs (and fetch fresh data). Use the dictionary approach when you already have the object and want to avoid a redundant lookup.
